From: defrancescojp <jpdefrancesco@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 10:12:26 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] 9675: Fix DynamicHash address byte count for segmented spaces
 (follow-up to #336, closes #9672)

Fix DynamicHash address byte count for SegmentedAddressSpace

DynamicHash.ToOpEdge.hash() was computing the CRC over the wrong
number of address bytes for x86:LE:16:Real Mode, causing
ActionDynamicMapping.findVarnode to never match equate symbols in
that language. The Java side used ceil(21/8)=3 bytes (from getSize()
with i+=8, introduced in the #336 fix); the C++ decompiler always
used 4 bytes because SleighLanguage encodes SegmentedAddressSpace as
32-bit when building the decompiler XML.

Add getAddressByteSize(Address) which returns 4 for SegmentedAddressSpace
(matching the SleighLanguage encoding) and (getSize()+7)/8 for all other
spaces (unchanged behavior). Update ToOpEdge.hash() to call the helper
with ++i.

Equates on CALLOTHER inputs (out, in, swi) now display correctly in
16-bit Real Mode. No change in behavior for other architectures.

Follow-up to #336
---
 .../program/model/pcode/DynamicHash.java      | 22 +++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Ghidra/Framework/SoftwareModeling/src/main/java/ghidra/program/model/pcode/DynamicHash.java b/Ghidra/Framework/SoftwareModeling/src/main/java/ghidra/program/model/pcode/DynamicHash.java
index 563f02ab14..9aca276f1d 100644
--- a/Ghidra/Framework/SoftwareModeling/src/main/java/ghidra/program/model/pcode/DynamicHash.java
+++ b/Ghidra/Framework/SoftwareModeling/src/main/java/ghidra/program/model/pcode/DynamicHash.java
@@ -19,6 +19,8 @@ import java.util.*;
 
 import generic.hash.SimpleCRC32;
 import ghidra.program.model.address.Address;
+import ghidra.program.model.address.AddressSpace;
+import ghidra.program.model.address.SegmentedAddressSpace;
 import ghidra.program.model.listing.Instruction;
 
 /**
@@ -106,8 +108,8 @@ public class DynamicHash {
 			reg = SimpleCRC32.hashOneByte(reg, slot);
 			reg = SimpleCRC32.hashOneByte(reg, transtable[op.getOpcode()]);
 			long val = op.getSeqnum().getTarget().getOffset();
-			int sz = op.getSeqnum().getTarget().getSize();
-			for (int i = 0; i < sz; i += 8) {
+			int sz = getAddressByteSize(op.getSeqnum().getTarget());
+			for (int i = 0; i < sz; ++i) {
 				reg = SimpleCRC32.hashOneByte(reg, (int) val);
 				val >>= 8;
 			}
@@ -222,6 +224,22 @@ public class DynamicHash {
 		opedge.clear();
 	}
 
+	/**
+	 * Return the number of address bytes to hash for an instruction's seqnum address.
+	 * This must match the addressSize the C++ decompiler uses, which is determined by
+	 * SleighLanguage encoding: SegmentedAddressSpace is encoded as 32 bits (4 bytes);
+	 * all other spaces use ceiling(getSize()/8).
+	 * @param addr is the seqnum target address
+	 * @return the number of bytes to include in the hash
+	 */
+	private static int getAddressByteSize(Address addr) {
+		AddressSpace spc = addr.getAddressSpace();
+		if (spc instanceof SegmentedAddressSpace) {
+			return 4;		// SleighLanguage encodes segmented spaces as 32 bits
+		}
+		return (spc.getSize() + 7) / 8;
+	}
+
 	/**
 	 * For a DynamicHash on a PcodeOp, the op must not be a CAST or other skipped opcode.
 	 * Test if the given op is a skip op, and if so follow data-flow indicated by the
